# Executive summary

## Main goal

Make the dual-sync system safe enough for production by ensuring that every import/export decision is:

```text
reviewed against fresh state
validated against field policy
executed section-aware
protected by locks
queued and throttled
audited with before/after hashes
recomputed after publish
observable and recoverable
```

## Why this plan focuses on hardening

The existing architecture already has the right core pieces: page rendering, OAuth connection, location linking, Google pull refresh, snapshot runs, dynamic registry, `dual_sync_field_states`, `dual_sync_outbound_candidates`, and `dual_sync_publish_operations`. 

The production risk is not that the system lacks architecture. The production risk is that bidirectional sync can accidentally become unsafe if field-level UI decisions are executed naively against Google APIs that behave at resource or section level.

Google’s Business Information `locations.patch` requires an `updateMask`, and supports `validateOnly` to validate requests without applying them. ([Google for Developers][1]) Attributes have their own `attributeMask`, and Google notes that available attributes vary by category/country and can change over time. ([Google for Developers][2]) Google also documents an edit limit of **10 edits per minute per Google Business Profile**, which cannot be increased. ([Google for Developers][3]) There is no sandbox environment for testing Business Profile API calls, so the production plan must include fake adapters, replay tests, and preflight validation where possible. ([Google for Developers][4])

